<%#
  Header for an organization page. `link_path` is where "back" goes -- the bank dashboard for an
  organization looking at itself, the admin dashboard for a super admin looking at one of many.

  The AdminLTE version carried a breadcrumb whose only real link was "Home"; the back link is the
  design system's equivalent and goes to that same one destination.

  **No wrapper of its own, and no `mb-0`.** This used to open a `px-4 py-6` block and then cancel
  the header's own bottom margin, while the details partial opened a *second* `px-4 py-6` -- so the
  gap between the heading and the first card measured 48px where the design system's is 24 (`mb-6`,
  the page header's default). It was the only view in the app overriding `wrapper_class`. The two
  `show` templates own the wrapper now, and everything on the page sits in it.

  **Edit lives here, not in the card.** It acts on the whole record rather than on one card's
  contents, which is the test in design.md. In the card header it read as editing that card's six
  fields rather than the record's twenty-eight.
%>
<% content_for :title, "Organizations - #{@organization.name}" %>

<%= render "shared/essentials/page_header",
      title: @organization.name,
      back: {path: link_path, label: "Back to dashboard"},
      actions: (can_administrate? ? edit_button_to(edit_organization_path, {size: "md"}) : nil) %>
